This study conducts statistical analysis and applies unsupervised machine learning algorithms to a dataset of facial tracking landmarks (FacialTracking_Actors_01-24), aiming to classify emotional states. The methodology encompasses data normalization, outlier detection via Z-scores, Boxplots, and DBSCAN, and feature extraction evaluating distances, angles, and areas between landmarks. To identify the most discriminative features for classifying eight distinct emotions, dimensionality reduction (PCA) and feature selection techniques (Fisher Score and ReliefF) were utilized. Finally, predictive modeling with Support Vector Machines (SVM) evaluated performance through Train-Test and Leave-One-Subject-Out (LOSO) cross-validation methods, highlighting the efficacy of different feature subsets.
